<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amorites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amorite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Amorites were a Semitic-speaking people of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, originally seen by Mesopotamian sources as nomadic outsiders. In the late third millennium BCE their migrations put pressure on the Akkadian and Neo-Sumerian empires. Amorite dynasties later ruled many Mesopotamian cities, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and an early Assyrian line under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amorites.jpg" alt="Amorites" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuneiform_Clay_Tablets_from_Amorite_Kingdom_of_Mari,_1st_Half_of_2nd_Mill._BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ur-Nanshe, 'son of Gu-NI.DU', left many inscriptions, nearly all about building temples; one records a channel dug against the <b>Amorites</b>, and several say he had the ships of Dilmun bring timber as tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 3rd and early 2nd millennia BCE the same gods appear in the personal names of the <b>Amorites</b>, whose homeland lay in the middle Euphrates and lower Khabur regions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
